{"title":"2009 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Many Gothic cathedrals have windows with portions containing a ring of congruent circles that are circumscribed by a larger circle, In the figure shown, the number of smaller circles is four. What is the ratio of the sum of the areas of the four smaller circles to the area of the larger circle? \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/c/a1c9cf43e5cccc1df5df071a00ccfbc0434fb45e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(6mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt));  draw(Circle((0,0),1+sqrt(2))); draw(Circle((sqrt(2),0),1)); draw(Circle((0,sqrt(2)),1)); draw(Circle((-sqrt(2),0),1)); draw(Circle((0,-sqrt(2)),1)); [/asy]\" width=\"138\" height=\"138\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/a/dbaeda9ef2d3f46fb63d792a4e3f94d0eb73e82b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A)}\\ 3-2\\sqrt2 \\qquad \\mathrm{(B)}\\ 2-\\sqrt2 \\qquad \\mathrm{(C)}\\ 4(3-2\\sqrt2) \\qquad \\mathrm{(D)}\\ \\frac12(3-\\sqrt2) \\qquad \\mathrm{(E)}\\ 2\\sqrt2-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"652\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw some of the radii of the small circles as in the picture below.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/1/941a0066171cfafbcecb4ec6ce35e0f2fc15db0b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(12mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt));  draw(Circle((0,0),1+sqrt(2))); draw(Circle((sqrt(2),0),1)); draw(Circle((0,sqrt(2)),1)); draw(Circle((-sqrt(2),0),1)); draw(Circle((0,-sqrt(2)),1)); draw( (sqrt(2),0) -- (0,sqrt(2)) -- (-sqrt(2),0) -- (0,-sqrt(2)) -- cycle ); draw( (0,sqrt(2)) -- (0,1+sqrt(2)) ); draw( (0,-sqrt(2)) -- (0,-1-sqrt(2)) ); draw( (0,sqrt(2)) -- (0,-sqrt(2)), dashed ); [/asy]\" width=\"278\" height=\"278\" />\n</p><p>Out of symmetry, the quadrilateral in the center must be a square. Its side is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a5745df50c8607bdc9b8de4968315633a3047dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />, and therefore its diagonal is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e21412832d95377643b75c0237ccc9f9828c944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" />. We can now compute the length of the vertical diameter of the large circle as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04206d065e79ee3f05c717db625ee0a56cf4e42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r + 2r\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/f/58ffa8ac5bdc10ea774c55da723776a775c23a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2R=2r + 2r\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/0/a80522710738811f1ab077d2ef176023399d4bf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=r+r\\sqrt{2}=r(1+\\sqrt{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"207\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p><p>Then the area of the large circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63ceb9ebfe0600252b064143b9e11a1fa42981a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L = \\pi R^2 = \\pi r^2 (1+\\sqrt 2)^2 = \\pi r^2 (3+2\\sqrt 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"326\" height=\"21\" />.\nThe area of four small circles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/a/29ab15020e6c60c817026fccec6d0354e587181e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = 4\\pi r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" />. Hence their ratio is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591c9365dd35ebdb87ba9f4566b29c7991b11173.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac SL  &amp; = \\frac{4\\pi r^2}{\\pi r^2 (3+2\\sqrt 2)} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac 4{3+2\\sqrt 2} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac 4{3+2\\sqrt 2} \\cdot \\frac{3-2\\sqrt 2}{3 - 2\\sqrt 2} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{4(3 - 2\\sqrt 2)}{3^2 - (2\\sqrt 2)^2} \\\\ &amp; = \\frac{4(3 - 2\\sqrt 2)}1 \\\\ &amp; = \\boxed{4(3 - 2\\sqrt 2)} \\end{align*}\" width=\"187\" height=\"287\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw the center of the large circle, and connect it with two adjacent centers of the smaller circles. Draw the line between the centers. Then, let the line connecting the center of the smaller circle to the center of the larger circle be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/3/fc3be04db73828946870d3ccc6d122395a4bacf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />\n</p><p>By the Pythagorean Theorem, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/f/93f6e39a56aaf2398e0706a74abb169d7515bc98.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + x^2 = (1+1)^2\\]\" width=\"142\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Simplifying, we have \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a1642bc6ea36b925ab17178875b8bb22f1efa8e9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x &amp;= \\sqrt{\\frac{(1+1)^2}{2}} \\\\ &amp;= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{2}} \\\\ &amp;= \\sqrt{2} \\end{align*}\" width=\"118\" height=\"118\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the radius is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/2/4a27d93e5c976d5687421abf367f03213611fe83.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + x = 1 + \\sqrt{2}\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The answer we desire is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f49c7ac2e42402772cde85a60b9fcc5e446255.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4 \\cdot \\left(\\pi \\cdot 1^2 \\right)}{\\pi \\cdot (1+\\sqrt{2})^2}\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>Simplifying, we have that \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/9/80937231d13aae3bf0f6442e5e0043762950b9b7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{4 \\cdot \\left(\\pi \\cdot 1^2 \\right)}{\\pi \\cdot (1+\\sqrt{2})^2} &amp;= \\frac{4 \\cdot \\pi}{\\pi \\cdot (1 + \\sqrt{2})^2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{\\pi \\cdot 4}{\\pi \\cdot (1+\\sqrt{2})^2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4}{(1+\\sqrt{2})^2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4}{1^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4}{1 + 2 \\sqrt{2} + 2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4}{3 + 2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4 (3 - 2\\sqrt{2})}{(3 + 2 \\sqrt{2})(3 - 2\\sqrt{2})} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4 (3 - 2\\sqrt{2})}{3^2 - (2\\sqrt{2})^2} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4 (3 - 2\\sqrt{2})}{9 - 8} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{4 (3 - 2\\sqrt{2})}{1} \\\\ &amp;= \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 4 (3 - 2\\sqrt{2})} \\end{align*}\" width=\"305\" height=\"535\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A\" title=\"2009 AMC 10A\">2009 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2009 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2009 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2009 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 20\">Problem 20</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2009 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2009_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_1\" tit